"Packing large balanced trees into bipartite graphs - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0012365X25002493)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Packing large balanced trees into bipartite graphs☆ ... We prove that for every $\\gamma >0$ there exists ${n}_{0}\\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that for every $n\\ge {n}_{0}$ any family of up to ${n}^{\\frac{1}{2}-\\gamma }$ trees having at most $(1-\\gamma )n$ vertices in each bipartition class can be packed into ${K}_{n,n}$. ... Packing and decomposition problems have a long history, naturally relating to Euler's 1782 question on the existence of orthogonal latin squares, and to the existence of designs. ... In 1963, Ringel [35] conjectured that a perfect packing of any tree into the complete graph ${K}_{m}$ for an appropriate m should always exist.\n\n## Discrete Mathematics\n\nDate: December 2025\n\nArticle: 114641\n\nVolume: Volume 348, Issue 12\n\nView accessibility information\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\nResearch article\n\nGet rights and content\n\n# Packing large balanced trees into bipartite graphs☆\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Cristina G. Fernandes ^{a} ^{1}, Tássio Naia ^{b} ^{2}, Giovanne Santos ^{c} ^{3}, Maya Stein ^{c} ^{4}\n\n\n10.1016/j.disc.2025.114641\n\nAccess through your organizationPurchase PDF\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article preview\n  * Recommended articles\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe prove that for every $\\gamma >0$ there exists ${n}_{0}\\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that for every $n\\ge {n}_{0}$ any family of up to ${n}^{\\frac{1}{2}-\\gamma }$ trees having at most $(1-\\gamma )n$ vertices in each bipartition class can be packed into ${K}_{n,n}$. As a tool for our proof, we show an approximate bipartite version of the Komlós–Sárközy–Szemerédi Theorem, which we believe to be of independent interest.\n\n## Keywords\n\nTree packing\n\n;\n\nGraph decomposition\n\n;\n\nBalanced trees\n\n## Introduction\n\nA family $\\mathcal{H}$ of graphs packs into a graph G if G contains pairwise edge-disjoint copies of all the members of $\\mathcal{H}$. The collection of these copies is called a packing of $\\mathcal{H}$ in G. Such a packing is perfect if it uses all edges of G. A graph H decomposes G if there is a perfect packing of a family consisting of multiple copies of H.\n\nPacking and decomposition problems have a long history, naturally relating to Euler's 1782 question on the existence of orthogonal latin squares, and to the existence of designs. We are interested in packing trees into complete bipartite graphs. We start our exposition with an overview of related problems and results, and then we describe our results, situating them within the literature.\n\nIn 1963, Ringel [35] conjectured that a perfect packing of any tree into the complete graph ${K}_{m}$ for an appropriate m should always exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPacking large balanced trees into bipartite graphs (https://repositorio.usp.br/directbitstream/092cfadd-2b83-48ac-924a-80106e5a1e28/3260446_-_Packing_large_balanced_trees_into_bipartite_graphs.pdf)\nciteturn2search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/disc\n\nPacking large balanced trees into bipartite graphs☆ ... In 1963, Ringel [35] conjectured that a perfect packing of any tree into the complete graph Kₘ for an appropriate m should always exist.\nDiscrete Mathematics 348 (2025) 114641\n\nContents lists available at ScienceDirect  \nDiscrete Mathematics  \njournal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/disc\n\nPacking large balanced trees into bipartite graphs☆\n\nCristina G. Fernandesᵃ,¹, Tássio Naiaᵇ,², Giovanne Santosᶜ,*,³, Maya Steinᶜ,⁴\n\nᵃ Instituto de Matemática e Estatística, Universidade de São Paulo, São Paulo, Brazil  \nᵇ Centre de Recerca Matemàtica, Bellaterra, Spain  \nᶜ Departamento de Ingeniería Matemática, Universidad de Chile, Santiago, Chile\n\nARTICLE INFO\n\nArticle history:  \nReceived 18 November 2024  \nReceived in revised form 25 April 2025  \nAccepted 5 June 2025  \nAvailable online xxxx\n\nKeywords:  \nTree packing  \nGraph decomposition  \nBalanced trees\n\nABSTRACT\n\nWe prove that for every γ > 0 there exists n₀ ∈ ℕ such that for every n ≥ n₀ any family of up to n^(1/2−γ) trees having at most (1 − γ)n vertices in each bipartition class can be packed into Kₙ,ₙ. As a tool for our proof, we show an approximate bipartite version of the Komlós–Sárközy–Szemerédi Theorem, which we believe to be of independent interest.\n\n© 2025 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.\n\n1. Introduction\n\nA family 𝓗 of graphs packs into a graph G if G contains pairwise edge-disjoint copies of all the members of 𝓗. The collection of these copies is called a packing of 𝓗 in G. Such a packing is perfect if it uses all edges of G. A graph H decomposes G if there is a perfect packing of a family consisting of multiple copies of H.\n\nPacking and decomposition problems have a long history, naturally relating to Euler’s 1782 question on the existence of orthogonal latin squares, and to the existence of designs. We are interested in packing trees into complete bipartite graphs. We start our exposition with an overview of related problems and results, and then we describe our results, situating them within the literature.\n\n1.1. Ringel-type conjectures\n\nIn 1963, Ringel [35] conjectured that a perfect packing of any tree into the complete graph Kₘ for an appropriate m should always exist.\n\n☆ This research was initiated at the first “ChiPaGra: Workshop Chileno Paulista em/en Grafos”, and was partially supported by the research project 2019/13364-7: “Extremal and Structural Problems in Graph Theory”, funded by FAPESP (The São Paulo Research Foundation) and ANID (Agencia Nacional de Investigación y Desarrollo).  \n* Corresponding author.  \nE-mail addresses: cris@ime.usp.br (C. G. Fernandes), tnaia@member.fsf.org (T. Naia), gsantos@dim.uchile.cl (G. Santos), mstein@dim.uchile.cl (M. Stein).  \n¹ CGF acknowledges partial support by CNPq (Proc. 423833/2018-9 and 310979/2020-0).  \n² TN was supported by the Grant PID2020-113082GB-I00 funded by MICIU/AEI. 10.13039/501100011033. This work is supported by the Spanish State Research Agency, through the Severo Ochoa and María de Maeztu Program for Centers and Units of Excellence in R&D (CEX2020-001084-M).  \n³ GS was supported by ANID Doctorado Nacional 21221049.  \n⁴ MS is also affiliated to Centro de Modelamiento Matemático, Universidad de Chile. The author was supported by ANID Regular Grant 1221905 and by ANID grant CMM Basal FB210005.\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.disc.2025.114641  \n0012-365X/© 2025 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPacking large balanced trees into bipartite graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.13290)\nciteturn2academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; Title: Packing large balanced trees into bipartite graphs\nTitle: Packing large balanced trees into bipartite graphs\nAuthors: Cristina G. Fernandes, Tássio Naia, Giovanne Santos, Maya Stein\nDate: Thu Oct 17 07:37:59 2024\n\nWe prove that for every ${\\gamma > 0}$ there exists $n_0 \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that for every ${n \\geq n_0}$ any family of up to $\\lfloor{n^{\\frac12+\\gamma}}\\rfloor$ trees having at most $(1-\\gamma)n$ vertices in each bipartition class can be packed into $K_{n,n}$. As a tool for our proof, we show an approximate bipartite version of the Koml\\'os-S\\'ark\\\"ozy-Szemer\\'edi Theorem, which we believe to be of independent interest.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSearch | arXiv e-print repository (https://owu.terracat.net/search/?__owu_origin_v1=aHR0cHM6Ly9hcnhpdi5vcmc&query=Rz%C4%85%C5%BCewski%2C+P&searchtype=author)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; A rainbow partition theorem for trees and connected maximin share allocations of chores ... We answer the question affirmatively… ▽ More Xiao, Qiu, and Huang (AAMAS 2023) and independently Lonc (personal communication) asked whether indivisible chores located at the vertices of a tree can always be allocated to $n$ agents in connected bundles so that the cost of every agent is at most its connected maximin share; for goods, this is a theorem of Bouveret, Cechlárová, Elkind, Igarashi, and Peters. ... The proof reduces the problem to inward partitions of oriented trees, which we obtain from the colourful KKM theorem on a simplex of edge weights, using a leaf-elimination labelling that remains compatible when weights vanish. ... Submitted 29 September, 2026; originally announced September 2026.\nWe answer the question affirmatively, even for monotone costs. The answer follows from a combinatorial theorem: if $\\mathcal P_1,\\ldots,\\mathcal P_k$ are partitions of the vertex set of a finite tree, each into at most $k$ connected parts, then the vertex set can be split into disjoint sets $B_1,\\ldots,B_k$, some possibly empty, such that each nonempty $B_i$ is connected and contained in a part of $\\mathcal P_i$. Equivalently, if each of $k$ colours occurs on at most $k-1$ edges of a tree, then the vertices can be partitioned into connected sets labelled by distinct colours, none containing an edge of its own colour; in particular, one can choose for every colour a component of the forest obtained by deleting that colour so that the chosen components cover the tree. The bound is already best possible for paths, and for additive costs, the theorem is equivalent to the fair-division statement. The proof reduces the problem to inward partitions of oriented trees, which we obtain from the colourful KKM theorem on a simplex of edge weights, using a leaf-elimination labelling that remains compatible when weights vanish. We also give an algorithm running in time $k^{O(k)}$ plus polynomial time. △ Less\n\nSubmitted 29 September, 2026; originally announced September 2026.\n\nMSC Class: 91B32; 91A10; 05C05; 05C15\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPacking large balanced trees into bipartite graphs | Article Information | J-GLOBAL (https://jglobal.jst.go.jp/en/public/202402210854187086)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; # Packing large balanced trees into bipartite graphs ... Basics of graph theory\n\n# Packing large balanced trees into bipartite graphs\n\n大規模平衡木の二部グラフへのパッキング〖JST機械翻訳〗\n\n  * Publisher site {{ this.onShowPLink() }} Copy service\n  * Access JDreamⅢ for advanced search and analysis.\n\nThis is a preprint. See \"About preprints\" page for more details.\n\nTo check the article posted on the arXiv has a history of withdrawal, see the primary information .\n\nAuthor (4)：\n\nFernandes Cristina G.\n\nAbout Fernandes Cristina G.\n\n  * Search \"202450001081928612\"\n  * Search \"Fernandes Cristina G.\"\n\n, \n\nNaia Tassio\n\nAbout Naia Tassio\n\n  * Search \"202450001085512437\"\n  * Search \"Naia Tassio\"\n\n, \n\nSantos Giovanne\n\nAbout Santos Giovanne\n\n  * Search \"202450001079407044\"\n  * Search \"Santos Giovanne\"\n\n, \n\nStein Maya\n\nAbout Stein Maya\n\n  * Search \"202450001081362597\"\n  * Search \"Stein Maya\"\n\nMaterial：\n\narXiv\n\nAbout arXiv\n\n  * Search \"O7000B\"\n\nPublication year： Oct. 17, 2024  update date on preprint server： Oct. 17, 2024\nJST Material Number： O7000B  Document type： Preprint\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Packing large balanced trees into bipartite graphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/385010597_Packing_large_balanced_trees_into_bipartite_graphs)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; Crawled: 